{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.TransposeSymmetricWeightsForceHalfH","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.TransposeSymmetricWeightsForceHalfH","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.trace_pair_sum_eq_h","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.trace_pair_sum_eq_h","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/TransposeSymmetricWeightsForceHalfH.lean#L125-L136","doc":"The eigenvalue-exchange hypothesis is not an assumption about Plancherel\nweights but an identity: if `λ` carries `m₊` copies of `q` and `m₋` of `−q⁻¹`,\nand `λ'` carries them the other way round, the normalised traces sum to `h`.\n\nStated for the raw multiplicities so the arithmetic is checkable, with `d` the\ncommon dimension `m₊ + m₋`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markov_param_indep_of_weights","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markov_param_indep_of_weights","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/TransposeSymmetricWeightsForceHalfH.lean#L108-L123","doc":"**Corollary: the value does not depend on which such scheme you pick.**\n\nTwo normalised transpose-symmetric weightings of the same trace data give the\nsame effective Markov parameter — so no search within that family can move\n`Z`, and hence (by `vHat_sub_vHat`) none can move V̂ either.  Recorded because\nthe natural response to a Φ_q pole is to reach for a different weight scheme,\nand this says which reaches are futile before any of them is coded. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markov_param_eq_half_h","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markov_param_eq_half_h","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/TransposeSymmetricWeightsForceHalfH.lean#L73-L106","doc":"**Transpose-symmetric normalised weights force `Z = h/2`.**\n\n`τ` is transposition on shapes (any involution would do — the proof uses only\nthat it is a bijection); `w` the weights; `t λ = tr(σ^λ)/dim λ`.\n\nThe hypothesis `ht` is the eigenvalue-exchange fact: transposing a shape swaps\nthe multiplicities of `q` and `−q⁻¹`, so the two normalised traces sum to\n`h = q − q⁻¹` regardless of what those multiplicities are.  Nothing else about\nthe representation is used.\n\n`two_ne` is not decoration: the argument pairs `λ` with `λ'` and halves, so in\ncharacteristic 2 there is no `h/2` to land on and the statement is empty.  It is\ncarried explicitly rather than as `[CharZero R]` because that is the whole of\nwhat the proof needs. ","signatureChunk":0}]}